 I'd be happy to help you create a React component for converting temperatures between different units using Hooks! Here's an example of how you might structure this component:

```javascript
import { useState } from 'react';

const TemperatureConverter = () => {
  // Define the initial state with Celsius as the default temperature unit
  const [temperature, setTemperature] = useState(25); // Initial value is in degrees Celsius
  const [unit, setUnit] = useState('celsius');

  // Function to handle temperature conversion based on selected unit
  const handleConvert = (newUnit) => {
    if (newUnit === 'fahrenheit') {
      setTemperature((prevTemp) => (prevTemp * 9 / 5 + 32));
      setUnit(newUnit);
    } else if (newUnit === 'kelvin') {
      setTemperature((prevTemp) => prevTemp + 273.15);
      setUnit(newUnit);
    }
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Temperature Converter</h2>
      <input
        type="number"
        value={temperature}
        onChange={(e) => setTemperature(parseFloat(e.target.value))}
      />
      <button onClick={() => handleConvert('fahrenheit')}>
        Convert to Fahrenheit
      </button>
      <button onClick={() => handleConvert('kelvin')}>Convert to Kelvin</button>
      <p>
        {temperature}{' '}
        {unit === 'celsius' ? '°C' : unit === 'fahrenheit' ? '°F' : 'K'}
      </p>
    </div>
  );
};

export default TemperatureConverter;
```

This component uses two state variables, `temperature` and `unit`, which are managed using the `useState` Hook. The `handleConvert` function handles the conversion logic based on the selected unit. The component renders an input field for entering the temperature value, buttons for selecting the target temperature unit (Celsius, Fahrenheit, or Kelvin), and displays the converted temperature value with its corresponding unit

