 I'd be happy to help you understand a logical syllogism with an example! A syllogism is a kind of logical argument consisting of two premises and a conclusion, where each premise and the conclusion have the same logical form. Here's an example of a classic syllogism:

[Begin Syllogism]
\begin{aligned}
\text{Premise 1:} &\quad \text{All men are mortal.} \\
\text{Premise 2:} &\quad \text{Socrates is a man.} \\
\text{Conclusion:} &\quad \text{Therefore, Socrates is mortal.}
\end{aligned}
[End Syllogism]

In this syllogism, the logical structure can be represented as follows:

[Begin Logical Structure]
\begin{aligned}
\text{(Universal Quantifier)} &\quad \forall x (\text{Man}(x) \rightarrow \text{Mortal}(x)) \\
\text{(Term Substitution)} &\quad \text{Substitute 'Socrates' for 'x'} \\
\text{Conclusion:} &\quad \therefore \text{Mortal(Socrates)}
\end{aligned}
[End Logical Structure]

