 To make the given code more efficient, I'd suggest the following steps:

1. Use `useMemo` to memoize the sorted prompts array to prevent unnecessary re-rendering of the component when the `prompts.data` changes.

```jsx
const sortedPrompts = useMemo(() => {
  if (!prompts.data) return [];
  return prompts.data.sort((a, b) => b.updatedAt.getTime() - a.updatedAt.getTime());
}, [prompts.data]);
```

2. Combine multiple state variables into one state object to reduce the number of state updates.

```jsx
const [promptState, setPromptState] = useState({
  selectedTargetPrompt: "",
  editedName: "",
  editedPrompt: "",
});
```

3. Use the destructured `setPromptState` in event handlers to simplify the code and avoid passing multiple functions as props to child components.

```jsx
<input
  value={promptState.editedName}
  onChange={(e) => setPromptState({ ...promptState, editedName: e.target.value })}
/>
```

4. Use `useCallback` to memoize functions that don't change often to optimize rendering.

```jsx
const handleDelete = useCallback(() => {
  void deletePrompt.mutateAsync({ id: promptState.selectedTargetPrompt });
  onClose();
}, [deletePrompt, onClose, promptState.selectedTargetPrompt]);

const handleSave = useCallback(() => {
  void upsertPrompt.mutateAsync({
    id: promptState.selectedTargetPrompt,
    name: promptState.editedName,
    prompt: promptState.editedPrompt,
  });
  onClose();
