2. File System: Store the images outside the database, in the file system, and keep only the file path or URL in MongoDB. This method is recommended for larger images and heavy traffic since serving static files is more efficient than querying a database. You may also use Amazon S3, Google Cloud Storage, Azure Blob Storage, etc., instead of the local file system for better scalability and availability.

3. Base64 Encoding: Another alternative is to encode the image into base64 format and then store it as a string within the document. However, this approach is not recommended due to the large increase in document size and slower query performance. It is suitable for small applications where data transfer between clients and servers is minimal.

